%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f0d9b320ba1bccb4f9fd7e7f2cb4c281, type: 3}
  m_Name: Sign-in Alert Mimic
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ScamCaseData
  caseId: C01
  queueNumber: A101
  caseTitle: Sign-in Alert Mimic
  scamType: Phishing Scam
  victimName: Alan Tan
  victimAge: 31
  victimOccupation: Manager
  initialComplaint: I received a security alert saying that someone had accessed
    my account. I followed the instructions in the email because I was worried that
    my account had been compromised.
  lobbyNpcPrefab: {fileID: 8067685855151852537, guid: 9e485c1c619baa7429b4ae17a9c7b301, type: 3}
  interviewNpcPrefab: {fileID: 8067685855151852537, guid: 9e485c1c619baa7429b4ae17a9c7b301, type: 3}
  behaviourType: 0
  interviewDecisions:
  - npcLine: I got a sign-in alert saying someone had accessed my account from another
      device. I panicked because I thought someone was already inside my account.
    choices: []
  - npcLine: The message said I had to secure my account immediately by clicking
      a link.
    choices:
    - playerChoice: What did the sign-in alert look like?
      npcResponse: It had the Google logo and said that a security risk had been
        detected. There was also a button asking me to check my account activity.
      unlockedFactIds:
      - google_logo
      - urgent_security_warning
      - check_activity_button
    - playerChoice: You clicked the link without checking whether the alert was real?
      npcResponse: I already told you it looked real. I was worried about my account
        and didn't really think about it at the time.
      unlockedFactIds:
      - urgent_security_warning
  - npcLine: When I clicked the button, it brought me to a page asking me to log
      in again.
    choices:
    - playerChoice: Did anything about the login page seem unusual?
      npcResponse: Looking back, the website address was slightly different from
        the actual Google address. But the page itself looked almost identical to
        the real login page.
      unlockedFactIds:
      - fake_login_page
      - suspicious_url
    - playerChoice: You entered your password again even though you were already
        signed in?
      npcResponse: "Yeah\u2026 the page said I needed to confirm my identity because
        of the suspicious sign-in."
      unlockedFactIds:
      - fake_login_page
  - npcLine: After I entered my password, I received another verification code and
      the page asked me to enter that too.
    choices:
    - playerChoice: What happened after you entered the verification code?
      npcResponse: "A little while later, I got a real notification saying my password
        had been changed. That\u2019s when I realised the first alert must have been
        fake."
      unlockedFactIds:
      - email_stolen
      - password_stolen
      - account_access
    - playerChoice: You gave them both your password and the verification code?
      npcResponse: I thought the code was part of securing the account. The whole
        thing was designed to make me feel like I had to act immediately.
      unlockedFactIds:
      - account_access
  - npcLine: The fake alert looked so similar to the real one. I only realised something
      was wrong after they already had access.
    choices:
    - playerChoice: Be more careful next time, check for suspicious links or signs
        before typing out your login details
      npcResponse: Yeah... Ill look more carefully next time, thank you.
      unlockedFactIds: []
  caseFileQuestions:
  - questionId: sign_alert_contact
    prompt: How did the scammer first contact the victim?
    options:
    - Security alert email
    - Phone call
    - Social media message
    - Delivery SMS
    correctOptionIndex: 0
    supportingFactId: urgent_security_warning
    explanation: The victim received a phishing email designed to imitate an official
      security alert.
  - questionId: sign_alert_warning
    prompt: Which feature made the email appear legitimate?
    options:
    - Google branding and security warning
    - A government logo
    - A delivery tracking number
    - A bank investment letter
    correctOptionIndex: 0
    supportingFactId: google_logo
    explanation: The scam email copied legitimate branding and security language
      to make the message appear trustworthy.
  - questionId: sign_alert_method
    prompt: Where did the email link send the victim?
    options:
    - Fake login page
    - Official bank website
    - Delivery tracking website
    - Government portal
    correctOptionIndex: 0
    supportingFactId: fake_login_page
    explanation: The phishing link redirected the victim to a fake login page designed
      to steal account credentials.
  - questionId: sign_alert_stolen
    prompt: What information was the scammer attempting to obtain?
    options:
    - Email and password
    - Delivery address only
    - School records
    - Passport photograph
    correctOptionIndex: 0
    supportingFactId: password_stolen
    explanation: The fake login page was designed to capture the victim's login details.
  howTheScamWorks: Scammers send phishing emails designed to look like official security
    alerts. The message usually creates urgency by claiming that suspicious activity
    has been detected. Victims are encouraged to click a link that leads to a fake
    login page where their account details can be stolen.
  warningSigns: "\u2022 Unexpected security warnings.\n\u2022 Urgent instructions
    asking you to take immediate action.\n\u2022 Suspicious website addresses.\n\u2022
    Links asking you to enter login details again.\n\u2022 Emails that imitate trusted
    companies using logos and branding."
  howToAvoid: Avoid opening account-security links directly from unexpected emails.
    Instead, manually open the official website or application and check your account
    from there. Always verify the website address before entering passwords or other
    sensitive information.
  whatToDoIfVictim: Immediately change the affected account password, enable additional
    security features where available, review recent account activity and contact
    the relevant service provider if unauthorised access is detected.
